(globalThis.TURBOPACK||(globalThis.TURBOPACK=[])).push(["object"==typeof document?document.currentScript:void 0,564658,e=>{"use strict";e.s(["FONT_3D_FAQ",0,[{question:"What is a 3D text generator?",answer:"A 3D text generator turns typed text into a three-dimensional mesh by extruding the letter shapes of a font. You type the text, pick a font, and adjust depth, bevel, and materials. The result is real 3D geometry you can rotate, export, and use in games, videos, logos, or 3D printing projects."},{question:"How do I make 3D text?",answer:"Type your text into the input field, choose one of the built-in fonts or upload your own, and adjust depth, bevel, spacing, and materials until it looks right. The 3D preview updates in real time. When you are happy with the result, export it as a GLB model or a transparent PNG render."},{question:"Which fonts can I use?",answer:"The tool ships with 15+ fonts covering clean sans-serifs, display styles, pixel fonts, and handwriting. You can also upload your own TTF or WOFF2 font file, so any font you have a license for works."},{question:"Is the 3D text generator really free?",answer:"Yes. It runs entirely in your browser with no signup, no credit cost, and no watermark. It is part of 3D AI Studio's collection of free tools."},{question:"Can I 3D print the text I create?",answer:"Yes, with one conversion step. Export your text as GLB, then convert it to STL, the format slicers expect. Blender imports GLB and exports STL for free, or you can use the format conversion tools in 3D AI Studio. Extruded lettering prints well because the shapes are solid and simple, which makes it a popular route for name plates, signs, and cake toppers."},{question:"Can I use the 3D text commercially?",answer:"The models you create are yours to use, including commercially. The one thing to check is the font license: the bundled fonts are open licensed Google Fonts, and for uploaded fonts you need the rights to use them in your project."},{question:"How do I get 3D letters into Blender or a game engine?",answer:"Export as GLB. Blender, Unity, Unreal Engine, and Godot all import GLB directly. The mesh arrives with its materials applied, so you can position, scale, and light it like any other asset."},{question:"Can I make a 3D logo from my text?",answer:"Yes. Wordmark logos work especially well: type the name, pick a font that matches the brand, add depth and a bevel, and export. The tool also imports SVG files, so an existing vector logo can be extruded the same way. For photo or raster logos, the image to 3D generator is the better fit."},{question:"What is the difference between 3D text and AI text-to-3D?",answer:"This tool extrudes fonts: the exact letters you type become 3D geometry, which is what you want for titles, signs, logos, and printed lettering. AI text-to-3D is different: you describe an object in words and an AI generates a model of that object. If you want a dragon, a chair, or any other object from a description, use the Text to 3D generator on 3D AI Studio."}],"GLB_COMPRESSION_FAQ",0,[{question:"What is GLB compression and why do I need it?",answer:"GLB compression reduces the file size of 3D models without significantly affecting visual quality. Large 3D files load slowly on websites, AR experiences, and mobile apps. Compressing your GLB files makes them load faster, use less bandwidth, and perform better in real-time applications like web viewers, game engines, and e-commerce product displays."},{question:"How much can I reduce my 3D model's file size?",answer:"The GLB compressor can reduce file sizes by up to 90% using a combination of Draco geometry compression and texture optimization. Typical results range from 50% to 90% reduction depending on the model. Textures often account for most of the file size, so texture compression (WebP, JPEG, or KTX2) provides the largest savings."},{question:"Will compression affect my model's visual quality?",answer:"With default balanced settings, the visual difference is minimal. The tool offers presets ranging from Minimal (preserves maximum quality) to Maximum (smallest file size). You can also fine-tune advanced settings like quantization bits, texture quality, and geometry simplification to find the right balance between file size and visual fidelity for your specific use case."},{question:"What compression methods does the tool use?",answer:"The compressor uses Draco compression for geometry (vertices, normals, UVs) and texture recompression (WebP, JPEG, or KTX2 formats) for image data. Additional options include geometry simplification, vertex welding to remove duplicates, configurable maximum texture size, and the ability to strip animations for static assets. All processing runs in your browser with no server upload required."},{question:"What file formats does the GLB compressor support?",answer:"The tool accepts GLB files as input and outputs compressed GLB files. If your model is in another format like OBJ, FBX, or GLTF, convert it to GLB first. You can also load models directly from a URL. Compressed outputs are compatible with all software and platforms that support GLB including three.js, Unity, Unreal Engine, and web AR viewers."},{question:"Is the GLB compressor free to use?",answer:"Yes. The basic GLB compression tool is free to use with no account required. Upload your file, select a preset or customize settings, and download the compressed result. Processing happens entirely in your browser so your files are never uploaded to a server. Pro features like loading models from Dashboard are available on paid plans."}],"IMAGE_TO_3D_FAQ",0,[{question:"What is image to 3D?",answer:"Image to 3D is AI technology that converts a regular 2D image into a fully textured 3D model. 3D AI Studio lets you turn any photo, sketch, or concept art into a detailed 3D asset in seconds. It's fast, intuitive, and requires no 3D modeling experience. Just upload an image and the AI handles the rest."},{question:"How do I convert an image to a 3D model?",answer:"Upload a JPG, PNG, or WebP image, choose an AI model like Prism 3.0 or Hunyuan 3.1 Pro, and click Generate. Your fully textured 3D model will be ready in as little as 20 seconds. Preview it in the built-in 3D viewer, then export as GLB, OBJ, FBX, STL, USDZ, BLEND, DAE, or 3MF."},{question:"Do I need 3D modeling experience or special software?",answer:"No. 3D AI Studio runs entirely in your browser with nothing to download or install. You don't need any experience with Blender, Maya, or other 3D software. Upload an image, pick an AI model, and click Generate. The built-in 3D viewer lets you preview and rotate your model before exporting it in your preferred format."},{question:"What images can I upload to generate a 3D model?",answer:"You can upload JPG, PNG, or WebP images. Photos, illustrations, concept art, sketches, and AI-generated images all work. For sketches or flat illustrations, we recommend editing them first in Image Studio to turn them into a stylized 3D-style render, which produces much better conversion results. For best results, use a clear image of a single object on a plain background with even lighting. Avoid cluttered backgrounds, heavy shadows, or multiple overlapping objects. You can also upload multiple views of the same object for better accuracy with multi-view models."},{question:"How long does image-to-3D generation take?",answer:"It depends on the AI model you select. Prism Turbo delivers high-quality results in 50-90 seconds. Prism 3.0 takes 2-4 minutes with state-of-the-art quality. Hunyuan 3.1 Pro takes 3-6 minutes for premium geometry with optional PBR materials. You can move any generation to the background and keep working."},{question:"What AI models can I choose from?",answer:"3D AI Studio offers 15+ AI models for image-to-3D conversion across every major provider: Prism 3.1 and Prism Turbo by Tripo, Tripo P1, Hunyuan 3.5, 3.1, and 3.0 by Tencent, Meshy 6, Rodin Gen-2.5 by Hyper3D, Hitem3D 2.1, Trellis 2 by Microsoft, and Seed3D by ByteDance. Each model specializes in different strengths from ultra-fast prototyping to production-grade mesh quality with PBR textures."},{question:"What 3D file formats can I export?",answer:"Export in GLB, OBJ, FBX, STL, USDZ, BLEND, DAE, or 3MF. Use GLB for web and AR, FBX for Unity and Unreal Engine, OBJ for Blender and Maya, STL or 3MF for 3D printing, USDZ for Apple AR, and BLEND for native Blender projects. With built-in retopology, you can also convert to clean quad meshes before exporting in any format."},{question:"Is image to 3D free to try?",answer:"Yes. New users receive free credits upon signing up, no credit card required. Free users can generate 3D models with select AI models. For access to all models, all export formats, faster queue priority, and premium features, check our paid plans on the Pricing page. Each generation uses a set number of credits depending on the AI model."},{question:"Can I enhance or edit the model after it's generated?",answer:"Yes. After generation, you can retopologize the mesh for clean quad topology, apply AI texture refinement, prepare models for multi-color 3D printing with vertex colors, segment the model into separate parts, and convert between formats. All post-processing tools are built in and no external software is needed."},{question:"Can I turn a photo into a 3D model?",answer:"Yes. 3D AI Studio lets you turn any photo into a fully textured 3D model. Upload a JPG, PNG, or WebP photo, select an AI model, and click Generate. The AI analyzes your photo and reconstructs it as a 3D mesh with textures in as little as 20 seconds. Photos of single objects on clean backgrounds produce the best results. You can also use multiple photos of the same object for more accurate geometry."},{question:"Is there an online image to 3D converter?",answer:"Yes. 3D AI Studio is a fully online image to 3D converter that runs in your browser with no downloads or installs. Upload an image, choose from 15+ AI models, and get a textured 3D model in seconds. Export in GLB, OBJ, FBX, STL, USDZ, BLEND, DAE, or 3MF. No GPU or 3D software required on your end - everything runs in the cloud."},{question:"How do I convert a picture to a 3D model?",answer:"Upload your picture to 3D AI Studio, pick an AI model like Prism 3.0 or Hunyuan 3.1 Pro, and click Generate. Your picture is converted into a fully textured 3D model you can rotate, inspect, and export. For best results, use a clear picture of a single object with even lighting. Sketches, illustrations, and AI-generated pictures all work. Export as GLB, OBJ, FBX, STL, or USDZ."}],"IMAGE_TO_PROMPT_FAQ",0,[{question:"What is image to prompt?",answer:"Image to prompt is an AI-powered tool that analyzes any image and generates a detailed text description you can use as a prompt for AI image generators. Upload a photo, illustration, or concept art and receive a rich description covering composition, style, colors, lighting, and subject matter. It reverse-engineers the creative intent behind an image into reusable text."},{question:"How does the AI describe my image?",answer:"The AI uses a vision-language model to analyze every aspect of your image including subject, style, composition, colors, lighting, textures, and mood. It then composes a natural-language description at your chosen length and detail level. You can focus the output on specific areas like materials, lighting, or artistic technique for more targeted prompts."},{question:"What image formats are supported?",answer:"You can upload PNG, JPG, and WebP images up to 5 MB. Photos, digital art, illustrations, concept art, screenshots, and AI-generated images all work well. For the most detailed descriptions, use clear images with good lighting and a visible subject. The tool processes any visual content, from simple sketches to complex scenes."},{question:"Can I customize the output prompt?",answer:"Yes. Advanced settings let you control prompt length from 10 to 450 words, choose a description style like detailed, concise, technical, artistic, or keywords-only, and select specific focus areas such as colors, materials, lighting, textures, composition, mood, or symbolism. You can also set the output language to English, Spanish, French, German, Italian, Japanese, or Chinese."},{question:"What are the description style options?",answer:"Default produces a balanced description suitable for most AI generators. Detailed gives maximum visual information. Concise delivers a short, focused prompt. Technical emphasizes precise visual attributes and measurements. Artistic highlights creative elements like mood, symbolism, and technique. Keywords outputs a comma-separated tag list ideal for Stable Diffusion and Midjourney."},{question:"Can I use the generated prompt for AI image generation?",answer:"Absolutely. The prompts are specifically designed for AI image generators like Midjourney, Stable Diffusion, DALL-E, and our own Image Studio. Copy the output with one click and paste it directly into any AI tool. Adjusting the description style and focus areas lets you tailor prompts for different generators and creative workflows."},{question:"What languages are supported for the output?",answer:"The tool supports eight languages: English, Spanish, French, German, Italian, Japanese, and Chinese, plus a default auto-detect mode. Select your preferred language in the advanced settings and the AI will generate the entire description in that language. This is useful for multilingual creative teams or when working with region-specific AI tools."},{question:"How many credits does image to prompt cost?",answer:"Each image-to-prompt generation costs 2 credits. New users receive free credits upon signing up with no credit card required. For higher volume usage and access to all platform features, check our subscription plans on the Pricing page. Credits are deducted only after a successful generation, never for failed attempts."},{question:"How accurate are the AI-generated descriptions?",answer:"The AI produces highly accurate descriptions of visible elements including objects, colors, composition, and style. It identifies artistic techniques, lighting setups, and material properties with strong precision. For abstract or highly stylized images, results may be more interpretive. You can improve accuracy by selecting specific focus areas that matter most for your use case."},{question:"Is image to prompt free to try?",answer:"Yes. Sign up for a free account and receive credits to try the tool immediately, no credit card required. Free users can generate prompt descriptions from any uploaded image. For unlimited access, faster processing, and all advanced features across the platform, explore our paid plans on the Pricing page."}],"IMAGE_TO_PROMPT_SEO_CONTENT",0,{sections:[{heading:"AI Image to Prompt Generator",content:"Upload any image and generate a detailed text description you can use as a prompt for AI image generators. 3D AI Studio's image-to-prompt tool uses a vision-language model to analyze composition, style, colors, lighting, materials, and subject matter - then writes a natural-language description at your chosen length and detail level. Perfect for reverse-engineering prompts from reference images for Midjourney, Stable Diffusion, DALL-E, or [Image Studio](/ImageGeneration). Supports PNG, JPG, and WebP uploads up to 5 MB. Free credits on signup, no credit card required."},{heading:"What You Can Do with Generated Prompts",subsections:[{heading:"Rapid Prototyping",content:"Upload a reference image, generate a prompt, then use it in [Image Studio](/ImageGeneration) to create variations. Iterate on visual concepts faster by letting the AI extract the creative intent from existing artwork."},{heading:"Asset Preparation",content:"Generate detailed descriptions of concept art and use them as prompts to create similar assets. Build consistent visual libraries by reusing the same prompt structure across multiple generations."},{heading:"Game Development",content:"Describe game screenshots or concept art to create prompt templates for batch-generating consistent environments, characters, and props across your project using AI image generators."},{heading:"3D Printing",content:"Describe physical objects or sculptures to generate prompts for creating similar 3D-printable models. Use the generated prompt in [Text to 3D](/TextTo3D) to produce a 3D model from the description."}]},{heading:"How Image to Prompt Works",content:"When you upload an image, the AI vision-language model processes it in multiple passes. First, it identifies the main subject, background, and overall composition. Then it analyzes visual properties like color palette, lighting direction, material textures, and artistic style.\n\nThe model composes a coherent text description that captures both obvious elements and subtle details. You control the output through four settings: prompt length (10-450 words), description style (default, detailed, concise, technical, artistic, or keywords), focus areas (colors, materials, lighting, textures, composition, mood, and more), and output language (8 languages supported).\n\nThe keywords style produces comma-separated tags ideal for Stable Diffusion and Midjourney. The artistic style emphasizes mood, symbolism, and creative technique. The technical style focuses on precise visual attributes. Each style is optimized for different AI generators and creative workflows.\n\nGenerated prompts work directly with [Image Studio](/ImageGeneration) for creating AI images, or with [Text to 3D](/TextTo3D) for generating 3D models from text descriptions. Copy any prompt with one click and paste it into your preferred tool."},{heading:"Tips for Best Results",subsections:[{heading:"Input Quality",content:"Use clear, well-lit images with visible subjects for the most accurate descriptions. Higher resolution images give the AI more detail to work with. Avoid heavily compressed, blurry, or very dark images."},{heading:"What to Avoid",content:"Images with text overlays, watermarks, or UI elements may produce descriptions that include those artifacts. Very abstract images produce more interpretive results. For the most accurate prompts, use images with a clear focal subject."}]},{heading:"Get Started",content:"Upload an image using the upload area above, adjust the description style and length to your needs, and click Generate Prompt. Copy the result and use it in [Image Studio](/ImageGeneration) for AI image generation, in [Text to 3D](/TextTo3D) for creating 3D models from descriptions, or paste it into any external AI tool like Midjourney or Stable Diffusion."}]},"REMESH_FAQ",0,[{question:"What is remeshing and why do I need it?",answer:"Remeshing is the process of rebuilding a 3D model's mesh with cleaner, more efficient polygon structure. AI-generated 3D models typically have messy, triangulated topology that makes them difficult to animate, rig, or edit. Remeshing converts these into clean quad or optimized triangle meshes, improving edge flow, reducing polygon count, and making models production-ready for games, animation, and 3D printing."},{question:"How do I remesh a 3D model online?",answer:"Upload a GLB file to the remesh tool, choose between quad or triangle remeshing, set your target polygon count and texture size, then click Remesh. The tool automatically rebuilds your mesh topology while preserving textures. No software installation required. The entire process runs in your browser and takes just a few seconds to complete."},{question:"What is the difference between quad and triangle remeshing?",answer:"Quad remeshing creates a mesh made entirely of four-sided polygons with clean, flowing edge loops. This is ideal for animation, rigging, and subdivision modeling. Triangle remeshing produces optimized triangulated meshes better suited for real-time rendering, game engines, and 3D printing. Quad remeshing costs 3 credits per model while triangle remeshing costs 1 credit."},{question:"What file formats does the remesh tool accept?",answer:"The remesh tool accepts GLB files as input. If your model is in OBJ, FBX, STL, or another format, you can convert it to GLB first using 3D AI Studio's format conversion tools in the Dashboard. After remeshing, the optimized model is exported with preserved textures and can be further converted to FBX, OBJ, BLEND, DAE, USDZ, or 3MF."},{question:"Can I control the polygon count after remeshing?",answer:"Yes. You can set a specific target polygon count before remeshing. The tool also offers options for texture size (up to 4096px resolution), texture format (JPEG or PNG), forced symmetry for symmetrical objects, and pivot centering. These controls let you optimize the mesh for your exact use case, whether that's a low-poly game asset or a high-detail cinematic model."},{question:"How does remeshing improve AI-generated 3D models?",answer:"AI-generated models from tools like Image to 3D and Text to 3D typically have dense, irregular triangle meshes. Remeshing cleans this up by creating uniform polygon distribution, proper edge flow for deformation, and reduced polygon counts for better performance. The result is a model that's easier to animate, rig, UV map, and import into professional 3D software like Blender, Maya, or game engines."},{question:"Is the remesh tool free to use?",answer:"Remeshing is available on all paid plans. Triangle remeshing costs 1 credit per model and quad remeshing costs 3 credits. The tool is accessible directly from the Remesh page or from your Dashboard after generating a 3D model. See the Pricing page for current plan details."}],"REMESH_SEO_CONTENT",0,{sections:[{heading:"AI 3D Model Remeshing & Retopology Tool",content:"Upload any GLB file and optimize its mesh topology with AI-powered remeshing. 3D AI Studio's remesh tool rebuilds polygon structure, reduces face counts, and converts messy triangle meshes into clean quad topology - all in your browser with no software to install. Choose from three remeshing engines: Prism for quad retopology with full texture control, Pro for multi-format output with triangle or quad options, and Hunyuan for AI-driven polygon reduction with optional texture baking. Models generated with [Image to 3D](/ImageTo3D) or [Text to 3D](/TextTo3D) can be remeshed directly from your [Dashboard](/Dashboard). Free credits on signup, no credit card required."},{heading:"Built for Real Workflows",subsections:[{heading:"Game Development",content:"Optimize AI-generated models for Unity and Unreal Engine. Reduce polygon counts to meet performance budgets, convert to quad topology for proper edge flow, and export as FBX or GLB ready for import into game engine material systems."},{heading:"3D Printing",content:"Clean up mesh topology for reliable slicing and printing. Create watertight geometry, reduce unnecessary detail, and export as STL or 3MF. Enable vertex color export for multi-color 3D printing workflows."},{heading:"Animation & Film",content:"Convert dense triangle meshes into clean quad topology with proper edge loops for deformation. Quad remeshing produces meshes that bend naturally at joints, making models ready for [AI rigging](/Tools/RiggingTool) and skeletal animation."},{heading:"Web & AR",content:"Compress and optimize 3D models for fast loading on websites and AR experiences. Reduce polygon counts and texture sizes while preserving visual quality. Export as GLB for web viewers or USDZ for Apple AR Quick Look."}]},{heading:"How AI Remeshing Works",content:"When you upload a GLB file, the AI analyzes the existing mesh structure and rebuilds it with optimized polygon distribution. The process preserves the model's visual shape while reorganizing how the surface is defined.\n\nTriangle remeshing redistributes faces evenly across the surface, eliminating dense clusters and sparse gaps. This improves rendering performance and produces more predictable behavior in physics engines and collision systems.\n\nQuad remeshing goes further by converting the entire surface into four-sided polygons arranged in flowing edge loops. Quad meshes are the standard in professional 3D work because they subdivide cleanly, deform predictably during animation, and give artists intuitive control when editing. The AI places edge loops to follow the model's natural surface curvature.\n\nTexture preservation rebakes the original surface appearance onto the new mesh topology. You control the output texture size (up to 4096px) and format (JPEG or PNG). Additional options include UV repacking, forced symmetry for symmetrical objects, scale adjustment, and pivot centering.\n\nFor models created in 3D AI Studio, the [Dashboard](/Dashboard) offers one-click remeshing optimized for AI-generated geometry. For external models from Blender, Maya, ZBrush, or other 3D software, use this upload tool."},{heading:"Tips for Best Remeshing Results",subsections:[{heading:"Input Quality",content:"Upload clean GLB files without corrupted geometry or overlapping faces. Models with a single mesh body produce the most consistent results. If your model has multiple separate objects, consider merging them in Blender before uploading."},{heading:"Polygon Settings",content:"Set your target face limit based on the intended use. Game assets typically need 5,000 to 15,000 faces. Cinematic models can go up to 50,000. Lower polygon counts run faster in real-time applications but lose fine surface detail."},{heading:"What to Avoid",content:"Extremely thin geometry like single-polygon planes and wire-like structures may not remesh well. Models with interior faces or non-manifold edges can produce artifacts. Very large file sizes over 100MB may time out during processing."},{heading:"Format Selection",content:"Choose FBX for game engines and animation software, GLTF for web applications, OBJ for maximum compatibility, STL for 3D printing, USDZ for Apple AR, and 3MF for multi-color printing. Each format preserves different aspects of the mesh data."}]},{heading:"Get Started",content:"Upload a GLB file using the upload area above, select a remeshing engine, adjust your settings, and click Remesh. For AI-generated models, we recommend starting with Prism using quad remeshing enabled for the cleanest topology. Need a 3D model first? Generate one with [Image to 3D](/ImageTo3D) from a photo, [Text to 3D](/TextTo3D) from a description, or create a reference image with [Image Studio](/ImageGeneration). After remeshing, your model is ready for [AI rigging](/Tools/RiggingTool), [mesh segmentation](/Tools/SegmentationTool), or direct export to your preferred 3D software."}]},"RIGGING_SEO_CONTENT",0,{sections:[{heading:"AI Auto-Rigging & Animation Retargeting Tool",content:"Add a skeleton rig to any 3D model and apply animations using AI. Upload a GLB file and the tool automatically analyzes body structure, places bones, computes skin weights, and generates a Mixamo-compatible rig - all without manual bone placement or weight painting. Choose from multiple rig types including biped, quadruped, hexapod, avian, serpentine, and aquatic. After rigging, apply preset animations like walk, run, jump, dance, and combat moves with one-click retargeting. Export as GLB or FBX for Unity, Unreal Engine, and Blender. Models generated with [Image to 3D](/ImageTo3D) or [Text to 3D](/TextTo3D) can be rigged directly from your [Dashboard](/Dashboard)."},{heading:"Built for Real Animation Workflows",subsections:[{heading:"Game Development",content:"Rig characters for Unity and Unreal Engine with Mixamo-compatible skeletons. Apply preset animations or retarget custom motion capture data. Export as FBX with baked animations ready for game engine import and blend tree setup."},{heading:"Animation & Film",content:"Add production-quality rigs to AI-generated characters for animated shorts, cinematics, and motion graphics. The Mixamo-compatible skeleton works with industry-standard animation tools and retargeting pipelines in Blender, Maya, and MotionBuilder."},{heading:"Rapid Prototyping",content:"Test character designs with animation before committing to manual rigging. Generate a character with [Text to 3D](/TextTo3D), rig it in minutes, and preview animations to validate proportions and silhouette before investing in production-quality manual rigs."},{heading:"VR & AR Experiences",content:"Rig characters for interactive VR applications and AR filters. The lightweight GLB export with embedded animations works directly in WebXR, three.js, and AR frameworks. Baked animations ensure consistent playback across devices and platforms."}]},{heading:"How AI Rigging Works",content:"The rigging workflow has three stages: analysis, rigging, and animation retargeting.\n\nDuring analysis, the AI examines your model's geometry to determine if it can be rigged and what body type it matches. It checks for clear limb separation, appropriate proportions, and a recognizable body structure. The analysis is free and takes about 30 seconds.\n\nIf the model passes analysis, the rigging stage places a bone hierarchy inside the mesh based on the detected body type. For bipeds, this includes spine, hips, legs, arms, hands, and head bones following the Mixamo bone naming convention. The AI then computes skin weights that determine how each vertex deforms when bones move, ensuring natural bending at joints.\n\nAfter rigging, you can optionally apply preset animations through retargeting. The tool maps standardized motion data onto your model's specific rig, adjusting for differences in proportions and bone lengths. Animations are baked into the export so they play back identically in any software that supports the output format.\n\nFor models created in 3D AI Studio, the [Dashboard](/Dashboard) offers integrated rigging with optimized defaults. For external models from Blender, Maya, or other 3D software, use this upload tool."},{heading:"Tips for Best Rigging Results",subsections:[{heading:"Model Preparation",content:"Upload models with clean, closed geometry and reasonable polygon counts. Models from [remeshing](/Tools/Remesh) produce the most consistent rigs because they have uniform polygon distribution and proper edge flow at joints. Avoid uploading raw sculpts with millions of polygons."},{heading:"Pose Requirements",content:"Humanoid models should be in a T-pose or A-pose with arms extended away from the body and legs slightly apart. This gives the AI clear separation between limbs for accurate bone placement. Models with arms at their sides or in action poses may produce less accurate rigs."},{heading:"What to Avoid",content:"Models with merged limbs, missing geometry, or extreme proportions may not rig correctly. Very thin appendages like tails, antennae, and long hair strands are difficult to rig automatically. Loose accessories like capes and belts should ideally be part of the main mesh."}]},{heading:"Get Started",content:"Upload a GLB file using the upload area above and click Run Analysis to check rigging compatibility. If your model passes, configure the rig type and output format, then click Start Rigging. After rigging completes, select a preset animation and apply it with one click. Need a character model? Generate one with [Image to 3D](/ImageTo3D) from a reference image or [Text to 3D](/TextTo3D) from a description. Use [Image Studio](/ImageGeneration) to create the perfect character concept art. Before rigging, consider running your model through [remeshing](/Tools/Remesh) for optimized topology that deforms better at joints."}]},"RIGGING_TOOL_FAQ",0,[{question:"What is AI rigging for 3D models?",answer:"AI rigging automatically adds a skeleton (armature) to a 3D model so it can be animated. Traditional rigging requires hours of manual bone placement, weight painting, and testing. AI rigging analyzes your model's geometry and places bones, assigns skin weights, and generates a control rig in minutes, making 3D animation accessible without specialized rigging knowledge."},{question:"How does the AI auto-rigging workflow work?",answer:"Upload a GLB file, and the tool first analyzes the model to check if it's suitable for rigging. It then automatically places bones based on the detected body type, computes skin weights for natural deformation, and generates a Mixamo-compatible rig. You can preview animations directly in the 3D viewer before exporting as GLB or FBX."},{question:"What types of characters and creatures can be rigged?",answer:"The tool supports multiple rig types including biped (humanoid characters), quadruped (four-legged animals), hexapod, octopod, avian (birds), serpentine (snakes), and aquatic creatures. Each rig type places bones appropriate for that body structure. For best results, models should have clear limbs and a recognizable body shape."},{question:"Does my model need to be in a specific pose?",answer:"For best results, humanoid models should be in a T-pose or A-pose with arms extended and legs slightly apart. This gives the AI clear limb separation for accurate bone placement. Models with arms at their sides or in complex poses may produce less accurate rigs. The analysis step will flag potential issues before rigging begins."},{question:"What file formats can I export after rigging?",answer:"Export rigged models as GLB or FBX. GLB works well for web applications, AR experiences, and three.js projects. FBX is the standard for Unity, Unreal Engine, and professional animation software like Blender and Maya. The exported rig is Mixamo-compatible, meaning you can apply Mixamo animations directly or retarget animations from other sources."},{question:"Is the AI rigging tool free to use?",answer:"AI rigging is available on paid plans. The tool uses credits from your 3D AI Studio subscription. The workflow includes model analysis, automatic rigging, and animation preview. You can rig models generated in 3D AI Studio or upload your own GLB files from external 3D software."}],"SEGMENTATION_SEO_CONTENT",0,{sections:[{heading:"AI Mesh Segmentation Tool for 3D Models",content:"Automatically separate a 3D model into logical parts using AI. Upload a GLB file and the segmentation tool identifies and isolates distinct components like limbs, clothing, weapons, accessories, and structural elements - no manual selection required. The segmented model is saved to your [Dashboard](/Dashboard) where you can view each part, toggle visibility, and download individual segments as separate GLB files. Models generated with [Image to 3D](/ImageTo3D) or [Text to 3D](/TextTo3D) can be segmented directly from your Dashboard. This tool is for external GLB files from Blender, Maya, ZBrush, or other 3D software."},{heading:"What Segmentation Enables",subsections:[{heading:"Game Development",content:"Isolate character parts for customization systems where players swap armor, helmets, weapons, and accessories. Each segment becomes an independent object that can be shown, hidden, or replaced at runtime in Unity or Unreal Engine."},{heading:"3D Printing",content:"Separate a complex model into individually printable parts. Print large models in sections that fit your build plate, assign different materials or colors to each segment, and assemble after printing for multi-material results."},{heading:"Selective Editing",content:"Edit specific parts of a model without affecting the rest. Retexture just the head, adjust the proportions of one limb, or replace a single component while keeping everything else unchanged. Export modified segments and reassemble."},{heading:"Asset Preparation",content:"Break down complex models into organized component libraries. Build reusable asset kits where individual parts can be mixed and matched across multiple projects. Segment once, reuse the parts in any combination."}]},{heading:"How AI Mesh Segmentation Works",content:"The AI analyzes your model's geometry to identify natural boundaries between distinct parts. It recognizes structural features like joints between limbs and torso, separation between clothing and skin, and boundaries between attached objects like weapons or accessories.\n\nThe segmentation process examines surface curvature, mesh connectivity, and geometric features to determine where one logical part ends and another begins. Unlike manual selection in 3D software, the AI processes the entire model at once and produces consistent, clean boundaries between segments.\n\nEach identified segment is assigned a unique color for visualization and exported as a separately addressable part within a single GLB file. You can then download the full segmented model or extract individual parts from your [Dashboard](/Dashboard).\n\nFor best results, upload models with a single continuous mesh and a single material slot. Models with multiple separate mesh objects or multiple materials may not segment as accurately. If your model needs cleanup before segmentation, run it through the [Remesh tool](/Tools/Remesh) first."},{heading:"Tips for Best Segmentation Results",subsections:[{heading:"Model Preparation",content:"Ensure your model is a single continuous mesh with one material slot. Models exported from sculpting tools like ZBrush may need to be merged into a single mesh in Blender before uploading. Clean, connected geometry produces more accurate segmentation boundaries."},{heading:"What to Avoid",content:"Models with multiple separate mesh objects, multiple materials, or non-manifold geometry may produce unexpected segmentation results. Very low-polygon models with fewer than 1,000 faces may not have enough geometric detail for the AI to identify distinct parts accurately."}]},{heading:"Get Started",content:"Upload a GLB file using the upload area above and click Start Segmentation. The AI will process your model in 90 to 120 seconds and save the segmented result to your Dashboard. Need a 3D model to segment? Generate one with [Image to 3D](/ImageTo3D) from a photo or [Text to 3D](/TextTo3D) from a description. Create the perfect reference image with [Image Studio](/ImageGeneration). After segmentation, individual parts can be further processed with [AI remeshing](/Tools/Remesh) for clean topology or [AI rigging](/Tools/RiggingTool) for animation."}]},"SEGMENTATION_TOOL_FAQ",0,[{question:"What is AI mesh segmentation for 3D models?",answer:"AI mesh segmentation automatically separates a 3D model into distinct logical parts using artificial intelligence. Instead of manually selecting and splitting geometry in software like Blender, you upload a GLB file and the AI identifies and isolates individual components such as limbs, clothing, weapons, and accessories. The result is a segmented model with independently controllable parts."},{question:"How do I segment a 3D model into parts?",answer:"Upload a GLB file with a single mesh and single material slot, select the model version, and click Start Segmentation. The AI analyzes the geometry and automatically separates it into logical parts in about 90 to 120 seconds. The segmented model appears in your Dashboard where you can view, toggle visibility, and download individual segments."},{question:"What file formats does the segmentation tool support?",answer:"The segmentation tool accepts GLB files as input. Your model must have a single mesh and a single material slot for accurate segmentation. The output is a segmented GLB file saved to your Dashboard. For models in other formats like OBJ, FBX, or STL, convert them to GLB first using the remesh tool's format conversion feature."},{question:"What can I do with segmented 3D model parts?",answer:"Segmented parts can be independently shown, hidden, exported, and manipulated. Common uses include isolating character armor pieces for game customization, separating limbs for rigging, extracting individual components for 3D printing, and selectively editing specific sections without affecting the rest of the model. Each segment exports as a standalone GLB file."},{question:"Does mesh segmentation work with game engines?",answer:"Yes. Exported segmented GLB files work with Unity, Unreal Engine, Blender, and all major 3D software and DCCs. Individual segments can be imported as separate objects for independent animation, material assignment, and LOD management. This is especially useful for character customization systems where players swap armor, weapons, or accessories."},{question:"Can I segment AI-generated 3D models?",answer:"Yes, but models generated in 3D AI Studio via Image to 3D or Text to 3D can be segmented directly from your Dashboard for a smoother integrated experience. This standalone tool is designed for external models uploaded as GLB files from Blender, Maya, ZBrush, or other 3D software. Both workflows use the same AI segmentation engine and produce identical results."},{question:"Is the AI mesh segmentation tool free to use?",answer:"Mesh segmentation is available on paid plans and costs 15 credits per model. The tool is accessible from the Segmentation page for external GLB uploads, or directly from your Dashboard for models generated in 3D AI Studio. See the Pricing page for current plan details and credit allowances."}],"TEXTURE_AI_FAQ",0,[{question:"What is AI texture generation for 3D models?",answer:"AI texture generation uses artificial intelligence to automatically create or replace textures on 3D models. Instead of manually painting textures in software like Blender or Substance Painter, you upload a 3D model, describe the look you want with a text prompt or reference image, and the AI generates fully textured results in seconds. 3D AI Studio supports PBR materials for realistic rendering in any game engine or 3D application."},{question:"How do I retexture a 3D model with AI?",answer:"Upload a GLB, FBX, or OBJ file, then choose a texturing mode. You can describe the texture you want with a text prompt, upload a reference image to match a specific style, or select from material presets. Click Generate and preview the result in the side-by-side 3D viewer comparing original and retextured versions. Download the finished model as a textured GLB."},{question:"What 3D file formats are supported for texturing?",answer:"You can upload GLB, FBX, or OBJ files. GLB works best and produces the fastest, most reliable results. FBX and OBJ are also supported but may require specific preparation. For optimal quality, use models with clean geometry and ideally under 100k faces. Models generated in 3D AI Studio can be textured directly from your Dashboard."},{question:"Can I control how the AI generates textures?",answer:"Yes. You have several options: type a text prompt describing the exact look you want, upload a reference image for style matching, adjust texture quality between Standard and Detailed, and enable PBR materials for physically accurate rendering. Multiple AI engines are available including Standard, Ultra, and Meshy, each producing different texture styles and quality levels."},{question:"What are PBR materials and does this tool support them?",answer:"PBR (Physically Based Rendering) materials simulate how real-world surfaces interact with light. They include texture maps for color, roughness, metallic properties, and surface detail. 3D AI Studio generates PBR-compatible textures that work directly in Unity, Unreal Engine, Blender, and other rendering software. This means your textured models look realistic under any lighting condition."},{question:"Do I need to manually UV unwrap my model first?",answer:"No. The AI handles texture mapping automatically, so manual UV unwrapping is not required before uploading. However, models with clean geometry and well-structured meshes produce better results. If your model was generated using 3D AI Studio's Image to 3D or Text to 3D tools, it's already optimized for texture generation and can be retextured directly from your Dashboard."},{question:"How much does AI texture generation cost?",answer:"Texture generation uses credits from your 3D AI Studio plan. The cost varies by AI engine and quality settings. Free users can try texture generation with select modes. Paid plans include more credits and access to all AI engines. You can texture models generated on the platform or upload your own files from external 3D software."},{question:"Is there an online AI texture generator?",answer:"Yes. 3D AI Studio is a fully online AI texture generator that runs in your browser with no downloads or installs. Upload a 3D model, describe the texture you want or provide a reference image, and get a retextured model in seconds. Supports PBR materials for realistic rendering in Unity, Unreal Engine, and Blender. No GPU or 3D software needed on your end."},{question:"Can I change the texture on an existing 3D model?",answer:"Yes. Upload any GLB, FBX, or OBJ file and the AI replaces or adds textures to it. You can retexture models from any source - 3D AI Studio generations, Blender exports, game assets, or downloaded models. Describe the new look with a text prompt, upload a style reference image, or choose from material presets. Preview before and after in the side-by-side 3D viewer."},{question:"Can AI automatically generate PBR textures for my 3D model?",answer:"Yes. 3D AI Studio automatically generates PBR textures including diffuse, roughness, metallic, and normal maps. The AI creates physically accurate materials that respond to lighting realistically in any game engine or renderer. Enable PBR mode when generating textures and the output includes all standard PBR maps ready for Unity, Unreal Engine, Blender, or any PBR-compatible workflow."}],"TEXT_TO_3D_FAQ",0,[{question:"What is text to 3D?",answer:'Text to 3D is AI technology that generates fully textured 3D models from written descriptions. With 3D AI Studio, just describe what you want, like "a stylized medieval chest with iron bands", and the AI builds a 3D asset you can rotate, preview, and export. No 3D modeling skills needed. For more control, you can also try generating an image first, then converting it to 3D.'},{question:"How do I create a 3D model from a text description?",answer:"Type a detailed description of the 3D model you want, select an AI model like Prism 3.0 or Meshy 6, and click Generate. The AI creates a fully textured 3D mesh from your prompt in as little as 20 seconds. Preview the result in the 3D viewer, then export as GLB, OBJ, FBX, STL, USDZ, BLEND, DAE, or 3MF."},{question:"Do I need any 3D experience to use text to 3D?",answer:"Not at all. 3D AI Studio is a browser-based tool with no downloads, no plugins, and no prior experience required. Just type a description, pick an AI model, and generate. The built-in 3D viewer lets you rotate and inspect the model from every angle before exporting. It's designed for everyone from beginners exploring ideas to professionals building production assets."},{question:"How do I write effective prompts for 3D generation?",answer:'Be specific about the object type, materials, and style. Describe a single object, not a full scene. A prompt like "a weathered wooden treasure chest with iron bands and a rustic lock" works much better than just "a chest." Mention your target use, whether game-ready, photorealistic, or 3D-printable. Avoid thin parts like wires and fingers, and skip interior details like mouth interiors.'},{question:"How fast is text-to-3D generation?",answer:"Speed varies by AI model. Prism Turbo creates draft models in 50-90 seconds for rapid prototyping. Prism 3.0 delivers state-of-the-art quality in 2-4 minutes. Meshy 5 and Meshy 6 take 5-10 minutes but produce excellent topology and textures. Hunyuan 3.1 Pro takes 3-6 minutes with optional PBR materials. All generations can run in the background while you keep working."},{question:"What AI models are available for text to 3D?",answer:"Choose from 14+ text-to-3D models including Prism 3.0 and Prism Turbo by Tripo, Hunyuan 3.1 Pro and Hunyuan 3.0 by Tencent, Forge Gen-2 and Forge Sketch by Deemos, and Meshy 4 through Meshy 6. Options range from Forge Sketch at 8 credits for instant low-poly concepts to Meshy 6 at 30 credits for production-quality assets."},{question:"Can I use AI-generated 3D models in commercial projects?",answer:"Yes. All 3D models generated on paid plans are fully owned by you with complete commercial rights. Use them in shipped games, published apps, client projects, marketing materials, videos, or 3D-printed products. They work with Unity, Unreal Engine, Blender, and all major 3D tools. The only limitation is reselling the raw, unmodified AI files directly as standalone assets."},{question:"Is the text-to-3D generator free?",answer:"You can start generating 3D models immediately with free credits, no credit card needed. Free accounts include access to select models for testing. For unlimited model selection, all export formats, and priority processing, see our paid plans on the Pricing page. Individual generations cost a set number of credits depending on the AI model and quality settings."},{question:"What can I do with the 3D model after generating it?",answer:"Preview and rotate it in the built-in 3D viewer, then export in your preferred format. You can also retopologize for clean quad meshes, refine textures with AI, segment the model into parts, prepare it for 3D printing with vertex colors, or send it directly to Blender. All tools are included and no additional software is required."},{question:"Can I turn a text description into a 3D model?",answer:'Yes. 3D AI Studio turns any text description into a fully textured 3D model. Just type what you want - a character, prop, vehicle, or any object - and the AI generates it in seconds. No 3D modeling skills or software needed. The tool runs entirely online in your browser. Try prompts like "a medieval stone tower with ivy" or "a futuristic drone with neon lights" to see it in action.'},{question:"Is there an online text to 3D converter?",answer:"Yes. 3D AI Studio is a fully online text to 3D converter that runs in your browser with no downloads or installs. Type a description, choose from 14+ AI models, and get a textured 3D model in seconds. Export as GLB, OBJ, FBX, STL, USDZ, BLEND, DAE, or 3MF. No GPU or 3D software required - everything runs in the cloud."},{question:"How do I generate a 3D model from a prompt?",answer:"Enter your prompt in the text field, select an AI model like Prism 3.0 or Meshy 6, and click Generate. The AI interprets your prompt and builds a fully textured 3D model you can rotate, inspect, and export. Be specific about the object, materials, and style for best results. Each generation takes 20 seconds to 6 minutes depending on the model you choose."}],"TUTORIALS_FAQ",0,[{question:"What tutorials are available in Learning Studio?",answer:"Learning Studio offers 50+ video tutorials covering every major feature of 3D AI Studio. Topics include the new node-based Flow tool, Image to 3D conversion, Text to 3D generation, Image Studio for creating reference images, Texture AI for retexturing models, retopology and remeshing workflows, 3D printing preparation, and Blender integration. Both official tutorials and community-created content are included, organized by difficulty level."},{question:"What is Flow and how do I learn it?",answer:"Flow is the node-based, infinite-canvas tool in 3D AI Studio where you wire together nodes - like text input, image generation, image-to-3D, edit, batch, and style reference - into custom pipelines, then promote them into reusable apps you can run from a simple form. The Flow tutorial series is a 7-part course that takes you from a blank canvas to building and sharing your own reusable AI workflows, including an episode on the AI Flow Builder agent that wires up flows from a single sentence."},{question:"What is the best tutorial for getting started?",answer:'Start with "Getting Started with 3D AI Studio" which covers the complete basics in about 6 minutes. From there, try the Image to 3D and Text to 3D tutorials to learn the core generation tools. For a full overview of the latest features, the v5.0 Complete Walkthrough covers the entire pipeline including Image Studio, Prism 3.0, retopology, and Blender integration.'},{question:"Are the tutorials free to watch?",answer:"Yes. All tutorials in Learning Studio are completely free with no account required. You can watch any lesson at your own pace and revisit them as often as you like. The library is regularly updated with new content covering recent features, workflow improvements, and community-requested topics."},{question:"Do I need prior 3D modeling experience to follow along?",answer:"No prior experience is needed. The beginner tutorials start from scratch and walk you through each step with clear explanations. Topics are organized by difficulty level so you can begin with the basics like uploading an image and generating your first model, then progress to advanced workflows like multi-view generation, retopology, and Blender pipelines as you build confidence."},{question:"What learning path do you recommend for new users?",answer:"Begin with the Getting Started guide, then move to Image to 3D for converting photos into 3D models. Next, explore Text to 3D for generating from descriptions, followed by Image Studio for creating better reference images. Once comfortable with generation, learn Texture AI for retexturing and the Remesh tool for optimizing meshes. The recommended tutorials are marked with a badge to help you follow the best learning order."},{question:"Do the tutorials cover advanced workflows and professional techniques?",answer:"Yes. Beyond beginner content, Learning Studio includes tutorials on complete production pipelines, multi-view 3D generation for improved accuracy, professional retopology and topology cleanup, 3D printing with vertex color baking, and end-to-end workflows from concept to game-ready or print-ready assets."},{question:"Can I request a tutorial on a specific topic?",answer:"Yes. Email support@3daistudio.com with your tutorial request, or suggest topics in the Discord community. The tutorial library grows regularly based on new feature releases, user feedback, and popular community requests. Community members are also welcome to submit their own video tutorials to share workflows and techniques with other users."}],"UV_UNFOLD_FAQ",0,[{question:"What is UV unfolding for 3D models?",answer:"UV unfolding creates a 2D layout of a 3D model's surface, mapping each polygon to a flat space where textures can be applied. Think of it like peeling the skin off a 3D object and laying it flat. This tool uses AI to automatically generate optimized UV maps without manual unwrapping in software like Blender or Maya."},{question:"What file formats does the UV unfold tool support?",answer:"Upload GLB, GLTF, FBX, or OBJ files up to 120 MB. GLTF files are automatically converted to GLB for processing. The tool outputs models with optimized UV layouts. For best results, use models with fewer than 30,000 faces. Models over this limit should be simplified with retopology first."},{question:"What is the face limit for UV unfolding?",answer:"The maximum face count is 30,000 faces. The tool automatically counts faces when you upload a model and shows a progress bar. If your model exceeds the limit, use the Remesh tool to reduce polygon count with retopology first, then upload the optimized model for UV unfolding."},{question:"Does UV unfolding preserve my existing textures?",answer:"No. UV unfolding generates new UV layouts only. Existing textures and materials are not preserved because the UV coordinates change during the unfolding process. After UV unfolding, you can apply new textures using the AI Texture tool or manually in your preferred 3D software."},{question:"How long does UV unfolding take?",answer:"UV unfolding typically takes 1 to 2 minutes depending on model complexity and face count. The tool shows real-time progress during processing. You can monitor the status in the processing modal. Simpler models with fewer faces process faster than complex ones."},{question:"How many credits does UV unfolding cost?",answer:"Each UV unfolding operation costs 5 credits. Credits are deducted only after processing begins successfully, never for failed attempts. Check your credit balance on your Dashboard. For higher volume usage, explore our subscription plans on the Pricing page."},{question:"Can I UV unfold models from my Dashboard?",answer:"Yes. You can select models directly from your Dashboard using the Choose from Dashboard button. This loads any model you have previously generated with Image to 3D or Text to 3D. You can also upload models from external sources like Blender, Maya, or ZBrush."}],"UV_UNFOLD_SEO_CONTENT",0,{sections:[{heading:"AI UV Mapping & Unfold Tool for 3D Models",content:"Automatically generate optimized UV layouts for your 3D models using AI. Upload a GLB, GLTF, FBX, or OBJ file and get back a model with clean, efficient UV mapping - no manual unwrapping required. The AI analyzes your model's geometry and creates UV islands that minimize distortion and maximize texture space utilization. Models generated with [Image to 3D](/ImageTo3D) or [Text to 3D](/TextTo3D) can be loaded directly from your [Dashboard](/Dashboard). Free credits on signup, no credit card required."},{heading:"What UV Unfolding Enables",subsections:[{heading:"Game Development",content:"Generate clean UV layouts for game assets. Properly unwrapped UVs are essential for texture baking, lightmap generation, and material assignment in Unity and Unreal Engine."},{heading:"3D Printing",content:"Prepare models for color 3D printing by creating UV maps that allow texture application. Combined with the AI Texture tool, you can add detailed surface colors to any model."},{heading:"Animation & Film",content:"Create production-ready UV layouts for character and prop texturing. Clean UVs with minimal distortion are critical for hand-painting textures or applying procedural materials."},{heading:"Rapid Prototyping",content:"Skip the tedious manual UV unwrapping step in your pipeline. Generate UVs automatically and move straight to texturing with [AI Texture](/Tools/TextureAI) or your preferred painting software."}]},{heading:"How AI UV Unfolding Works",content:"When you upload a 3D model, the AI analyzes the mesh geometry to identify natural seam locations where the surface should be cut for flattening. It considers surface curvature, polygon density, and geometric features to place seams in visually inconspicuous locations.\n\nThe unfolding process then flattens each UV island - a connected region of the surface - into 2D space while minimizing stretch and distortion. The AI optimizes the packing of these islands into a square texture atlas, maximizing the usable texture space.\n\nThe tool supports models up to 30,000 faces. If your model exceeds this limit, run it through the [Remesh tool](/Tools/Remesh) first to reduce polygon count while preserving visual quality. GLTF files are automatically converted to GLB format for processing.\n\nNote that UV unfolding generates new UV coordinates only. Existing textures and materials are not preserved because the mapping changes. After unfolding, apply new textures using [AI Texture](/Tools/TextureAI) or import the model into Blender, Maya, or Substance Painter for manual texturing."},{heading:"Tips for Best UV Results",subsections:[{heading:"Model Preparation",content:"Use models with clean, manifold geometry and no overlapping faces. Models from retopology produce the best UV results because they have uniform polygon distribution. Run the Remesh tool first if your model has messy topology."},{heading:"What to Avoid",content:"Models over 30,000 faces will be rejected. Very thin geometry, non-manifold edges, and disconnected floating vertices can cause UV artifacts. Complex interior geometry that is not visible from outside may waste texture space."}]},{heading:"Get Started",content:"Upload a 3D model using the upload area above or select one from your Dashboard. The tool will analyze the model, count faces, and prepare it for processing. Click Unfold UVs to generate optimized UV layouts. Need a 3D model first? Generate one with [Image to 3D](/ImageTo3D) from a photo, [Text to 3D](/TextTo3D) from a description, or create a reference image with [Image Studio](/ImageGeneration). After UV unfolding, apply textures with [AI Texture](/Tools/TextureAI) or [Remesh](/Tools/Remesh) for further optimization."}]},"VIDEO_GEN_FAQ",0,[{question:"What is AI video generation?",answer:"AI video generation uses artificial intelligence to create videos from text descriptions or images. Instead of filming or manually animating, you describe what you want or upload a reference image, and the AI produces a finished video clip. 3D AI Studio supports text-to-video, image-to-video, and multi-reference workflows with high-resolution output up to 4K and native audio on select models."},{question:"How do I create a video from text or images?",answer:"Open Video Studio and pick a mode. For text-to-video, write a detailed prompt describing the scene, action, camera angle, and mood. For image-to-video, upload an image and add a motion prompt describing how it should move. Choose a model, then click Generate. Your video is usually ready in about 30 seconds to a few minutes depending on the model, length, and resolution."},{question:"Can I turn a 3D model into a video?",answer:"Yes. You can render a showcase or turntable video of a 3D model directly with the GLB Video Renderer, which spins your model and exports a clean clip for product pages, portfolios, and pitches. For AI-driven motion, export a render of your model as an image and animate it with image-to-video in Video Studio. This pairs naturally with models you create in Image to 3D or Text to 3D."},{question:"Can I animate a 3D character with AI?",answer:"Yes. Take a clear render or image of your 3D character and use image-to-video with a motion prompt to bring it to life - gestures, walk-ins, expressions, and camera moves - while keeping the character on-model. To copy real movement, Kling Motion Control transfers the motion from a reference video onto your character image, so a static 3D character can dance, fight, or perform."},{question:"How does AI video fit into the 3D workflow?",answer:"Video lives alongside 3D in the same studio, so you can move between them in one pipeline. A common flow: generate or edit an image in Image Studio, animate it into a video here, and convert that same image into a 3D model with Image to 3D. You can also go the other way and render a finished 3D model into a video to show it off. Everything shares one account and one credit balance."},{question:"Can I make product and concept videos for my 3D designs?",answer:"Yes. Turn product shots, concept art, or 3D renders into short cinematic clips for marketing, store pages, game pitches, and design reviews. Generate the ideal input image in Image Studio, animate it into a product or concept video, and reuse the same asset as a 3D model - so one design becomes an image, a video, and a 3D model."},{question:"Which AI video models are available?",answer:"Video Studio includes a lineup of leading models you can switch between in one place: Google Veo 3.1 Lite, Veo 3.1 Fast, and Veo 3; Kling 3.0, Kling 3.0 Pro, Kling 2.6 Pro, Kling 2.5 Turbo, and Kling 2.1; Kling O1 for start and end transitions and Kling Motion Control for motion transfer; ByteDance Seedance 2.0 and Seedance 1.5; and Lucy 14B for ultra-fast drafts. Each model has its own strengths, speed, and pricing."},{question:"Which AI video model should I use?",answer:"Use Veo 3.1 for realistic results with audio and lip-sync, Kling 3.0 for long cinematic shots up to 15 seconds, Seedance 2.0 for multi-reference control and character consistency, and Lucy 14B for fast, low-cost drafts. If you are unsure, start with the default model for quick clips, then open the model picker to compare quality, speed, duration, and credit cost side by side."},{question:"What is the difference between Veo, Kling, and Seedance?",answer:"Veo (by Google) is known for photorealism plus native audio and lip-sync. Kling (by Kuaishou) excels at cinematic motion and longer shots up to 15 seconds, also with audio. Seedance (by ByteDance) is built for control, including multi-reference inputs for consistent characters and styles across shots. You can use all of them from the same studio without separate accounts."},{question:"Can I switch models without retyping my prompt?",answer:"Yes. Your prompt and any uploaded image stay in place when you switch models, so you can try the same idea across different models and compare results. The credit cost for the selected model and settings is always shown before you generate."},{question:"What is the difference between text-to-video, image-to-video, and reference-to-video?",answer:"Text-to-video creates a clip from a written prompt alone. Image-to-video animates a still image you upload using a motion prompt. Reference-to-video combines multiple inputs, such as images, videos, and audio, to guide character consistency, style, motion, and lip-sync across a shot. Most users start with image-to-video for predictable results."},{question:"What is reference-to-video and multi-reference generation?",answer:"Reference-to-video lets you combine up to 9 images, 3 videos, and 3 audio clips in a single generation. You reference them directly in your prompt using tags like @Image1, @Video1, and @Audio1. This enables consistent characters across shots, style transfer, motion transfer from a reference video, lip-sync to an audio track, and multi-shot editing."},{question:"Can I control camera movement and motion?",answer:"Yes. Describe camera moves in your prompt using terms like dolly in, pan left, crane shot, orbit, or handheld, along with the speed and intensity of motion. For precise motion, the Kling Motion Control model can transfer movement from a reference video onto your subject."},{question:"Can I set a start and end frame for my video?",answer:"Yes. Several models support an optional end frame so you can define how a clip begins and ends, and Kling O1 is built specifically for smooth start-to-end transitions. Upload your starting image, optionally add an end image, and the AI generates the motion in between."},{question:"Do AI-generated videos include sound?",answer:"Select models generate native audio, including ambient sound and dialogue. Veo and Kling models support audio generation, which you can toggle on or off in the settings. Audio generation can affect the credit cost, which is always shown before you generate."},{question:"Does it support lip-sync?",answer:"Yes. Veo models support lip-sync, so a character can speak in time with generated or referenced audio. In reference-to-video you can supply an audio clip and reference it in your prompt to drive the lip movement."},{question:"How long can the generated videos be?",answer:"Clip length depends on the model. Veo models generate up to 8 seconds, while Kling 3.0 and Seedance support longer durations up to about 15 seconds. Longer clips use more credits. You can select the exact duration in the settings before generating."},{question:"What resolutions and aspect ratios are supported?",answer:"Resolution goes up to 4K on Veo 3.1 Fast, with 1080p and 720p available across models. Aspect ratios include 16:9 for landscape, 9:16 for vertical social media, and 1:1 for square, plus an auto option that matches your input. This makes it easy to create content for YouTube, TikTok, Instagram, and the web."},{question:"What images work best for image-to-video?",answer:"Use clear, high-resolution images with good lighting and a well-defined subject. Sharp images with clean backgrounds animate more reliably than blurry or cluttered ones. If you need a better starting image, you can generate or edit one in Image Studio and send it straight to Video Studio."},{question:"How do I write a good prompt for video generation?",answer:'Be specific about the subject, action, environment, and camera movement, and include details like lighting, mood, and style. For example, "a golden retriever running through autumn leaves in slow motion, warm sunlight, shallow depth of field" works better than "a dog in a park." Keep each prompt focused on a single scene rather than many at once.'},{question:"Is there a tool to help me write prompts?",answer:"Yes. The built-in prompt helper can rewrite and expand your idea into a detailed, model-ready prompt using your text and any uploaded image. It is a quick way to get better motion and composition without learning prompt syntax."},{question:"How long does AI video generation take?",answer:"Most videos generate in about 30 seconds to a few minutes depending on the model, resolution, and length. Higher resolutions and longer clips take more time. Generation runs in the background, so you can set up your next video or keep working while it finishes, and all results appear in your Dashboard."},{question:"Where do my generated videos go?",answer:"Every video is saved to your Dashboard, where you can preview, download, and reuse it. Because generation runs in the background, you can queue several videos and come back to them later."},{question:"What video formats can I download?",answer:"Generated videos can be exported in common formats including MP4, MOV, and GIF, ready for editing software, social platforms, and web publishing. Full-resolution HD downloads without a watermark are available on paid plans."},{question:"How much does generating a video cost?",answer:"Video generation uses credits, and the cost varies by model, duration, resolution, and whether audio is enabled. The exact credit cost for your current settings is always shown before you generate, so there are no surprises. Faster, lighter models like Veo 3.1 Lite and Lucy 14B cost less than premium cinematic models."},{question:"Is there a free way to try AI video generation?",answer:"Yes. Free accounts get starting credits so you can try video generation. Free videos include a watermark and standard quality. Upgrading to a paid plan unlocks the highest-quality models, HD downloads without a watermark, and faster renders."},{question:"Do free videos have a watermark?",answer:"Videos generated on the free plan include a watermark. On paid plans the watermark is removed and you can download in full HD. Paid plans also unlock the premium and 4K models."},{question:"Can I use AI-generated videos for commercial projects?",answer:"Yes. Videos generated on paid plans are fully owned by you with commercial rights. Use them in marketing campaigns, social media content, YouTube videos, product showcases, client presentations, or any commercial project."},{question:"Can I animate an image I created or edited with AI?",answer:"Yes. Image Studio includes 100+ AI tools to generate and edit images, remove backgrounds, upscale, and more. Once your image looks right, send it directly to Video Studio to animate it, creating a smooth image-to-video workflow in one place."},{question:"Can I turn my videos or images into 3D models?",answer:"3D AI Studio also offers image-to-3D and text-to-3D generation. You can create a concept image, animate it into a video, and generate a matching 3D model, all within the same platform, which is useful for games, product design, and 3D content."},{question:"How do I make AI videos for TikTok, Reels, and YouTube Shorts?",answer:"Select the 9:16 vertical aspect ratio and a short duration, then write a prompt or upload an image for your clip. Vertical, fast-paced clips work best for short-form platforms. You can also generate 16:9 for standard YouTube and 1:1 for square feeds from the same studio."},{question:"How do I keep a character consistent across multiple clips?",answer:"Use reference-to-video and supply the same reference image of your character, referencing it in each prompt. Models like Seedance 2.0 are designed for multi-reference consistency, so your character keeps the same look, outfit, and style across different shots."},{question:"My video does not match my prompt. How do I fix it?",answer:"Make your prompt more specific about the subject, action, camera movement, and style, and keep it to a single scene. Try the prompt helper to expand your idea, switch to a higher-quality model, or for image-to-video start from a clearer input image. Small wording changes and a better model often make a big difference."}],"VIEWER_3D_FAQ",0,[{question:"What is the 3D AI Studio Viewer?",answer:"The 3D Viewer is a free online tool that lets you open and inspect 3D models directly in your browser. It supports GLB, GLTF, FBX, OBJ, and STL files with full orbit, zoom, and pan controls. No installation, plugin, or sign-up is required, and your files never leave your device. It works on desktop, tablet, and mobile."},{question:"Which 3D file formats can I view?",answer:"The viewer opens GLB, GLTF, FBX, OBJ, and STL files. GLB and GLTF carry embedded materials and textures and are the recommended formats for the web. FBX preserves embedded textures and animations. OBJ files load with optional MTL and texture images. STL files are geometry-only and ideal for 3D printing inspection."},{question:"Can I view textured OBJ and FBX models?",answer:"Yes. For FBX, embedded textures inside the binary file load automatically. For OBJ, drop the .obj together with its .mtl file and any texture images (PNG, JPG, WebP) at the same time and the viewer will resolve them. You can also apply a single override texture to any model from the sidebar to quickly try a different look."},{question:"Is the 3D viewer free to use?",answer:"Yes. The 3D Viewer is completely free, requires no account, and has no upload limits beyond your browser's memory. Models stay on your device, nothing is uploaded to a server. If you need to generate, retopologize, texture, or convert 3D models you can use the rest of the 3D AI Studio platform with free credits on signup."},{question:"How do I open a GLB file online without Blender?",answer:"Drag your GLB file onto the viewer or click the upload area. The model loads instantly and you can rotate, zoom, and pan it with the mouse or touch. There's no need to install Blender, Maya, or any other 3D software. GLB files are the web-native 3D format and load fastest because materials and textures are packed into a single file."},{question:"How do I open an STL file in the browser?",answer:"Drop your STL file onto the viewer to open it instantly. STL files are geometry-only with no colors or textures, so the viewer applies a clean clay material by default. Use the sidebar to switch to wireframe, X-ray, or normal-view materials, change the surface color, and inspect the geometry from any angle before sending it to your 3D printer."},{question:"Can I see the wireframe and polygon count?",answer:"Yes. Toggle Wireframe in the sidebar to overlay the mesh structure with a custom color. The Stats panel shows live triangle count, vertex count, draw calls, and FPS. This is useful for inspecting topology, checking if a model is too dense for a game engine, or verifying a remesh result before exporting from another pipeline."},{question:"Can I change the lighting and HDRI environment?",answer:"Yes. Pick from five built-in HDRI environments to instantly change the lighting mood, from neutral studio to dramatic sunset. Toggle the HDRI as the visible background, adjust environment intensity, and tweak ambient and key light strength. You can also choose between ACES, AGX, or Linear tone mapping for cinematic, film, or technical looks."},{question:"Does the viewer work on mobile?",answer:"Yes. The viewer is fully responsive and works on iOS and Android. Use one finger to orbit, two fingers to pinch-zoom, and three fingers (or two-finger drag) to pan. The settings panel collapses into a floating button on small screens so the model gets the full viewport. Touch performance scales with your device, so heavier models load faster on newer phones."},{question:"Can I take a screenshot of the model?",answer:"Yes. Click the Screenshot button in the sidebar to download a high-resolution PNG of the current view, including your background color or HDRI. Screenshots use the canvas's native pixel ratio for crisp output, so the result is suitable for portfolio shots, store listings, presentations, or sending preview renders to clients without exporting from a renderer."},{question:"Are my files uploaded anywhere?",answer:"No. The viewer is fully client-side. Your model files are read directly in your browser using object URLs and are never sent to a server. This keeps proprietary or unreleased designs private and makes loading instant even for large GLB or FBX files. You can verify this by opening DevTools and watching the Network tab while you load a model."},{question:"What's the difference between this and a Blender or game engine viewer?",answer:"Blender, Maya, Unity, and Unreal are full editors with steep learning curves. The 3D AI Studio Viewer is a focused inspection tool that opens any common 3D format in seconds. Use it to quickly preview downloads, share link-free renders with teammates, verify exports, or check models before importing them into a heavier 3D application."},{question:"Can I view models I created with 3D AI Studio?",answer:"Yes. Models you generated with [Image to 3D](/ImageTo3D) or [Text to 3D](/TextTo3D) can be viewed here by dragging the downloaded GLB onto the viewer. For an integrated experience, your full library is also available in the [Dashboard](/Dashboard) where each model has its own viewer. Use this tool when inspecting external files or files from other software."},{question:"How large can my model be?",answer:"There is no fixed file size limit. The practical limit depends on your device's memory. Most modern computers handle GLB files up to 200 MB and FBX files up to 500 MB without issues. For very dense meshes above one million triangles, consider running them through the [Remesh tool](/Tools/Remesh) first to reduce polygon count for smoother viewing."},{question:"Can I rotate the model automatically?",answer:"Yes. Enable Auto-Rotate in the Camera section of the sidebar to slowly orbit the model. Adjust the rotation speed with the slider. This is useful for showcase videos, presentations, social media clips captured with screen recording, or simply admiring your work without touching the mouse. Disable auto-rotate any time you want to inspect a specific angle."}],"VIEWER_3D_SEO_CONTENT",0,{sections:[{heading:"Free Online 3D Model Viewer",content:"Open and inspect any 3D model directly in your browser with the 3D AI Studio Viewer. Drop a GLB, GLTF, FBX, OBJ, or STL file onto the viewport and the model loads instantly with full orbit, zoom, and pan controls. No accounts, no uploads to a server, no plugins, no installations. The viewer is completely free and runs 100% client-side, so your files stay on your device. Need to create or edit 3D content first? Generate models from [photos](/ImageTo3D), [text descriptions](/TextTo3D), [retopologize](/Tools/Remesh) imported meshes, or [convert formats](/Tools/Remesh) using the rest of 3D AI Studio."},{heading:"Built for Inspecting Any 3D Format",subsections:[{heading:"View GLB & GLTF",content:"GLB is the web-native 3D format with materials, textures, and animations packed into a single file. The viewer uses the same Khronos-standard loader as Sketchfab, model-viewer, and Babylon.js so what you see here is exactly what other GLB viewers see."},{heading:"View FBX",content:"FBX files commonly arrive from Autodesk Maya, 3ds Max, Cinema 4D, and many marketplace stores. The viewer parses binary FBX, loads embedded textures automatically, and centers the model so you don't have to fight the import scale."},{heading:"View OBJ",content:"OBJ is the universal exchange format for Blender, ZBrush, MeshLab, and most CAD tools. Drop the .obj alone for clay preview, or together with its .mtl file and texture images to view the model with its full original shading."},{heading:"View STL",content:"STL is the standard for 3D printing. The viewer renders STL meshes with a smooth clay material so you can verify silhouettes and surface finish before slicing, then switch to wireframe to inspect topology issues that could cause print failures."}]},{heading:"Why Use a Browser-Based 3D Viewer",content:"Heavyweight 3D applications like Blender, Maya, and Unreal Engine are powerful but slow to launch and have steep learning curves. When all you need to do is preview a downloaded model, verify a client deliverable, or share a quick render with teammates, opening a desktop editor is overkill.\n\nThe 3D AI Studio Viewer fills that gap. Drop any GLB, FBX, OBJ, or STL file onto the page and it appears in under a second with smooth orbit, zoom, and pan controls. Switch between materials, lighting setups, and HDRI environments to see how the model performs under different conditions.\n\nBecause the viewer runs entirely in your browser using WebGL and three.js, your model files never leave your device. This is critical for proprietary game assets, unreleased product designs, and client work under NDA. There is no upload step, no server processing, and no waiting in queues. You can confirm this by watching the network tab while you load a model. The only network request is for the viewer's own JavaScript bundle.\n\nThe viewer also doubles as a quality control tool for your AI generations. Models created with [Image to 3D](/ImageTo3D), [Text to 3D](/TextTo3D), or after running through [Remesh](/Tools/Remesh) can be downloaded as GLB and re-opened here to confirm they look right outside the generation environment, with the same renderer most engines use."},{heading:"Customization & Inspection Tools",subsections:[{heading:"HDRI Environments",content:"Switch between five hand-picked HDRI environments to instantly change the lighting mood. Toggle the HDRI as the visible background or keep it for lighting only and use a solid color or transparent background for clean studio shots."},{heading:"Material Modes",content:"Beyond the original textures, switch the model to wireframe with a custom color, clay for silhouette analysis, X-ray to see hidden geometry, or matcap-style normals to debug surface direction. Toggle modes without re-uploading the file."},{heading:"Camera & Auto-Fit",content:"Use jump-to-view shortcuts for front, back, sides, top, bottom, and perspective. Auto-fit re-centers the camera on the model bounding box. Auto-rotate spins the object slowly so you can capture turntable-style screen recordings without touching the mouse."},{heading:"Mesh Statistics",content:"The Stats panel reports vertex count, triangle count, draw calls, and live FPS. Use it to verify a model fits a game engine budget, to compare topology before and after [retopology](/Tools/Remesh), or to spot heavy meshes that need optimization."}]},{heading:"How the Viewer Loads Each Format",content:"When you drop a file onto the viewport, the viewer detects the format from its extension and routes it to the matching loader. GLB and GLTF files are parsed with the official Khronos GLTFLoader, which reads embedded buffers, materials, textures, and animations. FBX files are parsed with FBXLoader, which understands both binary and ASCII FBX revisions and decodes embedded texture data when present.\n\nOBJ files use OBJLoader. If you drop the .mtl file and referenced texture images at the same time, MTLLoader resolves the material library and remaps relative texture paths to in-browser blob URLs through a custom URL modifier. This means OBJ exports from Blender, ZBrush, or any modeling tool render with their original shading without manual setup. You can also drop a single .obj for a fast geometry-only preview.\n\nSTL files use STLLoader. Because STL is a triangle-soup format with no colors or texture coordinates, the viewer applies a high-quality default material and centers the geometry. Smooth shading is computed automatically for organic shapes while the original facets are preserved for hard-surface and engineering parts.\n\nEvery loaded model is automatically centered and scaled to fit a reference frame. The camera is positioned to frame the bounding box, and you can re-center at any time with the auto-fit shortcut. This avoids the common headache of importing a model and finding it tiny, gigantic, or off-camera."},{heading:"Tips for Best Viewing Results",subsections:[{heading:"Use GLB When Possible",content:"GLB is the fastest and most accurate format for the web. Materials, textures, and animations are packed into one file with no path resolution headaches. Most modeling tools can export GLB, and you can convert from FBX or OBJ using free tools or 3D AI Studio's own export pipeline."},{heading:"Drop OBJ With Its MTL & Textures",content:"If your OBJ uses external materials, select the .obj, .mtl, and texture files together when dropping or browsing. The viewer reads them as a group and resolves all references in-memory. Without the MTL, the OBJ renders as untextured clay which is still useful for geometry inspection."},{heading:"Reduce Heavy Meshes First",content:"Models above 1–2 million triangles can stutter on integrated GPUs and mobile devices. Use the [Remesh tool](/Tools/Remesh) to reduce polygon count while preserving silhouette and texture quality, then re-open the result here for smooth interaction at full frame rate."},{heading:"Match Background to Asset",content:"Light or stylized assets read better against a dark background, while dark or moody assets pop against neutral studio HDRIs. Try a few backgrounds before screenshotting. The viewer keeps your camera angle stable when you swap environments so you can compare looks fairly."}]},{heading:"Get Started",content:"Drag a GLB, GLTF, FBX, OBJ, or STL file onto the viewport above to start, or pick one of the sample models to explore the controls. Use the right-side sidebar to switch HDRI environments, toggle wireframe, change materials, frame the camera, and download a high-resolution screenshot. Need to create the perfect 3D model first? Generate one from a [photo](/ImageTo3D), a [text prompt](/TextTo3D), or [optimize an existing model](/Tools/Remesh) for animation, printing, or game engines. All processed models export back to GLB, FBX, OBJ, STL, USDZ, BLEND, DAE, or 3MF and re-open right here in the viewer."}]}])}]);

